    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a loft addition for a residential project up in Oregon. I'm looking at using a Douglas Fir beam for the main span. The span is 14 feet and I'm expecting a dead load of about 15 psf plus a live load of 30 psf. Can you help me figure out what size beam I'll need to keep deflection within L/360 limits?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For a 14-foot span with a combined load of 45 psf, you will need to focus on checking the beam's modulus of elasticity ($E$) and moment of inertia ($I$) to satisfy deflection limits. Considering standard structural practices for Douglas Fir, a deep timber beam is usually necessary to control deflection over this span. You should consult your local Oregon building codes and use an approved load and strength chart or structural software to determine the exact nominal size required for L/360 compliance.",
      "source": "target",
