    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i'm trying to avoid a massive overkill on the size if possible. assuming i go with something like an 8x12 or a 6x14 to start, are there any specific things i should be watching out for in the wood itself that might mess with the load capacity or cause unexpected sagging later on? like way beyond just basic checking for knots?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Beyond simple knots, you must closely examine the wood's **grade and moisture content**; these directly affect its allowable strength and stiffness. Ensure the Douglas Fir has been properly dried to meet industry standards in Oregon to prevent shrinkage and warping later. Also, check for any signs of fungal decay, insect damage, or checks/splits perpendicular to the grain, as these significant defects drastically reduce load-bearing capacity.",
      "source": "target",
